##! The stage-0 prelude: the builtin types and the prelude functions of
##! proposal/02-syntax.md ("Prelude functions and intrinsics") and the parts
##! of std.core that Phase 2 needs.
##!
##! Every function without a body names a host intrinsic with
##! `@intrinsic("name")`. The `##` comment above it is the intrinsic's
##! contract, stated in Sisu terms: a Sisu-written std or another
##! interpreter must behave exactly so. `@keeps(self, x)` adds to the
##! contract that every value the named `own` and `mut` arguments hold is
##! kept or returned, never dropped, so they may hold must-consume values
##! (D-054). A function with a body and `@structural("name")` is written in
##! Sisu; the intrinsic `name` computes the same result when the bounds of
##! its type arguments are met by structural impls, and lowering calls it
##! then. `@builtin("name")` marks a type whose
##! values the host represents; such a type has no fields unless it lists
##! them, and code constructs it only through its associated functions.
##!
##! The traits the language uses (`Display`, `Debug`, `Eq`, `PartialOrd`,
##! `Ord`, `Hash`, `Len`, `Copy`, `Drop`, `Iterator`, the operators) are
##! declared here, with their impls for the builtin types. An impl whose
##! methods are intrinsics named `structural.*` implements the trait the
##! way the interpreter compares, hashes and prints values structurally
##! (docs/architecture.md, "Traits and generics"). `Iterable[T]` in a
##! parameter accepts a `list`, `set`, `dict` or `Iter` whose items are `T`.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ host-backed types

## An owned, growable UTF-8 string. Views as `str` wherever a `str` is expected.
@builtin("String")
struct String:
    pass

## A growable sequence, the type of `[a, b]`.
@builtin("list")
struct list[T]:
    pass

## A hash map iterating in insertion order (D-074), the type of `{k: v}`.
@builtin("dict")
struct dict[K, V]:
    pass

## A hash set iterating in insertion order, the type of `{a, b}`.
@builtin("set")
struct set[T]:
    pass

## An iterator the host drives: over a collection, a range, the pieces of
## a string. Its adapters (`map`, `filter`, `take`, ...) are `Iterator`'s,
## written in Sisu below, and run their functions only as items are taken.
@builtin("Iter")
struct Iter[T]:
    pass

## A slot of a dict, from `dict.entry`.
@builtin("Entry")
struct Entry[K, V]:
    pass

## Anything a `for` loop can iterate whose items are `T` (a parameter type only).
@builtin("Iterable")
struct Iterable[T]:
    pass

## A file system path. A string literal becomes a `Path` where one is expected.
@builtin("Path")
struct Path:
    pass

## A point in civil time, in UTC.
@builtin("DateTime")
struct DateTime:
    pass

## The file system capability (06). A `Copy` handle.
@builtin("Fs")
struct Fs:
    pass

## The environment capability: arguments and variables.
@builtin("Env")
struct Env:
    pass

## The clock capability.
@builtin("Clock")
struct Clock:
    pass

## The root of all capabilities, constructed by the runtime only when
## `main(sys: System)` declares it, and passed to `main` alone.
@builtin("System")
struct System:
    fs: Fs
    env: Env
    clock: Clock

## An owned value on the heap (04, rule 7). Methods and fields of the value
## are reached through it (its own field, `boxed`, is the value), and a
## `Box[dyn Trait]` holds a value of any type that implements the trait.
struct Box[T]:
    boxed: T

## Shared ownership (04, rule 5): a reference-counted pointer to a value
## no one may change through it. `.copy()` makes another owner, which costs
## a count increment, not a copy of the value; the value is dropped with
## its last owner. Changing it goes through a `Cell` or a `Mutex` inside. A
## view cannot be shared (it would outlive what it borrows), and neither can
## a must-consume value (no owner is the last one statically). Whether the
## count is atomic is a flag the runtime keeps; stage 0 has one task, so it
## is never set (the escape analysis that sets it comes with tasks, 06).
@builtin("Shared")
struct Shared[T]:
    pass

## A handle on a `Shared` value that does not keep it alive; it breaks the
## cycles a graph of `Shared` would leak (04, rule 5).
@builtin("Weak")
struct Weak[T]:
    pass

## A value replaced through a shared reference, in one task (04, rule 5):
## no reference into it is ever given out, so replacing it invalidates none.
@builtin("Cell")
struct Cell[T]:
    pass

## Mutual exclusion over a value the mutex owns (06, "Send, Sync, and shared
## state"): `lock` gives a guard, `guard.value` is the value, and dropping
## the guard unlocks. Stage 0 has one task, so `lock` on a mutex its own
## task holds panics (it would wait forever). When unwinding drops a guard,
## the value's type's `invariant` is checked again (D-077): if it fails, the
## mutex is poisoned and every later `lock` panics; if it holds, or the type
## has none, the mutex unlocks.
@builtin("Mutex")
struct Mutex[T]:
    pass

## The lock on a `Mutex`, from `Mutex.lock`; `guard.value` is the value.
@builtin("MutexGuard")
struct MutexGuard[T]:
    pass

## A collection of `T` addressed by `Key[T]` handles (04, "What this
## costs, honestly"): `insert` gives a key; `remove` frees the slot, which a
## later `insert` reuses under a new generation. A key whose value was
## removed is stale: `get` gives `None` and `arena[key]` panics, so a stale
## handle is detected at use, never dangling.
@builtin("Arena")
struct Arena[T]:
    pass

## A handle into an `Arena[T]`: the slot and its generation when the value
## was inserted.
@derive(Copy, Eq, Hash)
struct Key[T]:
    index: int
    generation: int

## Where `Display` and `Debug` write: the text being built and the format
## spec (`{x:>8.2}`) of the value being rendered. The parts of a value are
## written at a greater `depth`, where the spec does not apply to them. A
## leaf value written at depth 0 applies the whole spec itself and clears
## it; what is left of it pads the whole text when the interpolation ends.
struct Formatter:
    out: String
    spec: String
    depth: int

impl Formatter:
    ## Appends `text`.
    pure def write(mut self, text: str):
        self.out.push(text)

## What `Hash` feeds a value's parts into.
struct Hasher:
    state: int

impl Hasher:
    ## Mixes an integer into the state (FNV-1a over its value).
    pure def write_int(mut self, n: int):
        self.state = (self.state ^ n) *% 1099511628211

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ traits

## Text for a program's users: what `print` and `"{x}"` write (03, "Strings").
trait Display:
    ## Writes the value's text to `f`.
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter)

## Text for programmers: what `"{x:?}"` writes, the value the way it is
## written. Every type has `Debug` (11): a struct or enum without its own
## impl gets the derived one.
trait Debug:
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

    ## The derived impl (08, "Derive"), which the compiler runs for a type:
    ## a struct's name and its fields as `name=value`, an enum value's
    ## variant and its fields, the way they are written.
    comptime def derive(T: type) -> impl Debug for T:
        return impl Debug for T:
            pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter):
                if comptime reflect.is_struct(T):
                    f.write("{reflect.name_of(T)}(")
                    f.depth += 1
                    comptime for i, field in enumerate(reflect.fields_of(T)):
                        if comptime i > 0:
                            f.write(", ")
                        f.write("{field.name}=")
                        self.[field].fmt_debug(mut f)
                    f.depth -= 1
                    f.write(")")
                elif comptime len(reflect.variants_of(T)) > 0:
                    match self:
                        comptime for V in reflect.variants_of(T):
                            case V(..):
                                if comptime len(V.fields) == 0:
                                    f.write(V.name)
                                else:
                                    f.write("{V.name}(")
                                    f.depth += 1
                                    comptime for i, field in enumerate(V.fields):
                                        if comptime i > 0:
                                            f.write(", ")
                                        if comptime field.named:
                                            f.write("{field.name}=")
                                        self.[field].fmt_debug(mut f)
                                    f.depth -= 1
                                    f.write(")")

## Equality: `==` and `!=` (02, "Expressions").
trait Eq:
    pure def eq(self, other: Self) -> bool

    ## The derived impl: equal when the variants are and the fields are.
    comptime def derive(T: type) -> impl Eq for T:
        return impl Eq for T:
            pure def eq(self, other: Self) -> bool:
                if comptime reflect.is_struct(T) or len(reflect.variants_of(T)) == 0:
                    comptime for field in reflect.fields_of(T):
                        if self.[field] != other.[field]:
                            return False
                    return True
                else:
                    match self:
                        comptime for V in reflect.variants_of(T):
                            case V(..):
                                match other:
                                    case V(..):
                                        comptime for field in V.fields:
                                            if self.[field] != other.[field]:
                                                return False
                                        return True
                                    comptime if len(reflect.variants_of(T)) > 1:
                                        case _:
                                            return False

## A partial order: `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`. `f64` has only this one, since
## NaN is unordered (03, "Numbers").
trait PartialOrd:
    pure def partial_cmp(self, other: Self) -> Ordering?

## A total order, for sorting, `min` and `max`.
trait Ord(Eq, PartialOrd):
    pure def cmp(self, other: Self) -> Ordering

## Hashing, for `dict` keys and `set` elements.
trait Hash(Eq):
    pure def hash(self, mut h: Hasher)

    ## The derived impl: the fields' hashes in order, after the variant's
    ## index for an enum.
    comptime def derive(T: type) -> impl Hash for T:
        return impl Hash for T:
            pure def hash(self, mut h: Hasher):
                if comptime reflect.is_struct(T):
                    comptime for field in reflect.fields_of(T):
                        self.[field].hash(mut h)
                elif comptime len(reflect.variants_of(T)) > 0:
                    match self:
                        comptime for V in reflect.variants_of(T):
                            case V(..):
                                h.write_int(V.index)
                                comptime for field in V.fields:
                                    self.[field].hash(mut h)

## A number of items, `len(x)`.
trait Len:
    pure def len(self) -> int

## Values that are copied rather than moved (04, rule 1); `@derive(Copy)`
## opts a type in when all its fields are `Copy`.
@marker
trait Copy:
    pass

## The numbers `sum` and `abs` work on.
@sealed
trait Num:
    pass

## Runs when a value's owner goes out of scope, before its fields are
## dropped (04, rule 6).
trait Drop:
    def drop(mut self)

## The items of a `for` loop (03, "Traits"). `Iter[T]` is the iterator the
## standard library's methods return; the adapters below wrap an iterator
## in another, and the consumers drive it to its end (or to the item they
## look for). As in 03, `next` and the methods are not `pure`, and their
## functions may be any (`(Item) -> U`): an impl's `next` may be `pure`
## (the host iterators' is), and a `pure` function can loop over one.
trait Iterator:
    type Item
    ## The next item, or `None` at the end.
    def next(mut self) -> Item?

    ## At most `n` items.
    def take(own self, n: int) -> Take[Self]:
        return Take(inner=self, left=n)

    ## All but the first `n` items.
    def skip(own self, n: int) -> Skip[Self]:
        return Skip(inner=self, left=n)

    ## Each item passed through `f`.
    def map[U](own self, f: (Item) -> U) -> Map[Self, U]:
        return Map(inner=self, f=f)

    ## The items for which `keep` is `True`.
    def filter(own self, keep: (ref Item) -> bool) -> Filter[Self]:
        return Filter(inner=self, keep=keep)

    ## Each item with its index, from 0.
    def enumerate(own self) -> Enumerate[Self]:
        return Enumerate(inner=self, index=0)

    ## The first item for which `pred` is `True`.
    def find(own self, pred: (ref Item) -> bool) -> Item?:
        for x in self:
            if pred(ref x):
                return Some(x)
        return None

    ## Whether `pred` is `True` for every item; stops at the first `False`.
    def all(own self, pred: (Item) -> bool) -> bool:
        for x in self:
            if not pred(x):
                return False
        return True

    ## Whether `pred` is `True` for any item; stops at the first.
    def any(own self, pred: (Item) -> bool) -> bool:
        for x in self:
            if pred(x):
                return True
        return False

    ## The number of items.
    def count(own self) -> int:
        var n = 0
        for _ in self:
            n += 1
        return n

    ## `f` applied to the running value, from `init`, and each item in turn.
    def fold[B](own self, own init: B, f: (B, Item) -> B) -> B:
        var acc = init
        for x in self:
            acc = f(acc, x)
        return acc

    ## The items in a new list.
    def collect(own self) -> list[Item]:
        var out: list[Item] = []
        for x in self:
            out.push(x)
        return out

    ## The item with the largest `key`; the first of equal ones.
    def max_by[K: Ord](own self, key: (ref Item) -> K) -> Item?:
        var best: Item? = None
        for x in self:
            # The key may borrow from its item, so the best one's is
            # computed again rather than kept.
            let better = match best:
                case Some(b): key(ref x) > key(b)
                case None: True
            if better:
                best = Some(x)
        return best

    ## The item with the smallest `key`; the first of equal ones.
    def min_by[K: Ord](own self, key: (ref Item) -> K) -> Item?:
        var best: Item? = None
        for x in self:
            let better = match best:
                case Some(b): key(ref x) < key(b)
                case None: True
            if better:
                best = Some(x)
        return best

    ## The sum of the items, as `sum` adds them (floats pairwise, D-074).
    def sum(own self) -> Item where Item: Num:
        return self.collect().sum()

    ## The `Display` renderings of the items, separated by `sep`.
    def join(own self, sep: str) -> String where Item: Display:
        return self.collect().join(sep)

## `a + b` (D-022). The other operators are `Sub`, `Mul`, `Div`, `Rem` and
## `Neg` (`-a`).
trait Add[Rhs = Self]:
    pure def add(self, other: Rhs) -> Self

trait Sub[Rhs = Self]:
    pure def sub(self, other: Rhs) -> Self

trait Mul[Rhs = Self]:
    pure def mul(self, other: Rhs) -> Self

trait Div[Rhs = Self]:
    pure def div(self, other: Rhs) -> Self

trait Rem[Rhs = Self]:
    pure def rem(self, other: Rhs) -> Self

trait Neg:
    pure def neg(self) -> Self

## A type that literals can be written as (02, "Literals"; 03, "Numbers"):
## a literal whose expected type implements `FromLiteral[L]` is
## `from_literal` of the literal, where `L` is `int` for an integer literal,
## `f64` for a float literal and `str` for a string literal without
## interpolation. It applies to literals only; it is not a conversion of
## values.
trait FromLiteral[L]:
    pure def from_literal(literal: L) -> Self

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ core enums and error sets

## `T?` is `Option[T]`.
@derive(Eq, Ord, Hash)
enum Option[T]:
    Some(T)
    None

## `T ! E` is `Result[T, E]`.
@derive(Eq, Ord, Hash)
enum Result[T, E]:
    Ok(T)
    Err(E)

@derive(Copy, Eq, Ord, Hash)
enum Ordering:
    Less
    Equal
    Greater

## The error `Result.context` wraps another in (05, "Context and traces"):
## what the program was doing, and the error it met, rendered with
## `Display`. It displays as `message: cause`.
error Contextual:
    Context(message: String, cause: String)

## The errors of the file system capability.
error IoError:
    NotFound(path: Path)
    PermissionDenied(path: Path)
    AlreadyExists(path: Path)
    InvalidData(path: Path)
    Interrupted
    Other(message: String)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ prelude functions

## Writes the `Display` rendering of `x` and a line break to standard output.
@intrinsic("print")
def print[T: Display](x: T)

## Writes the `Display` rendering of `x` and a line break to standard error.
@intrinsic("print_err")
def print_err[T: Display](x: T)

## The number of items: Unicode scalar values for `str` and `String`,
## elements for `list` and `set`, entries for `dict`.
@intrinsic("len")
pure def len[T: Len](x: T) -> int

## `range(stop)` is `0, 1, ..., stop - 1`; `range(start, stop)` and
## `range(start, stop, step)` count by `step`, which must not be zero, and stop
## before reaching `stop`. With one argument the checker supplies `start = 0`.
@intrinsic("range")
pure def range(start: int, stop: int, step: int = 1) -> Iter[int]

## Pairs each item with its index, from 0.
@intrinsic("enumerate")
pure def enumerate[T](xs: Iterable[T]) -> Iter[(int, T)]

## Pairs the items of `a` and `b`; panics if they have different lengths (PEP 618).
@intrinsic("zip")
pure def zip[A, B](a: Iterable[A], b: Iterable[B]) -> Iter[(A, B)]

## Pairs the items of `a` and `b` until the shorter one ends.
@intrinsic("zip_shortest")
pure def zip_shortest[A, B](a: Iterable[A], b: Iterable[B]) -> Iter[(A, B)]

## The items in reverse order.
@intrinsic("reversed")
pure def reversed[T](xs: Iterable[T]) -> Iter[T]

## A new list of the items in ascending order; the sort is stable.
@intrinsic("sorted")
pure def sorted[T: Ord](xs: Iterable[T]) -> list[T]

## The smaller of `a` and `b`; `a` when they are equal.
@intrinsic("min")
pure def min[T: Ord](a: T, b: T) -> T

## The larger of `a` and `b`; `a` when they are equal.
@intrinsic("max")
pure def max[T: Ord](a: T, b: T) -> T

## The sum of the items, 0 for none; integer overflow panics. Floats are summed
## pairwise in a fixed shape (D-074).
@intrinsic("sum")
pure def sum[T: Num](xs: Iterable[T]) -> T

## Whether any item is `True`; stops at the first.
@intrinsic("any")
pure def any(xs: Iterable[bool]) -> bool

## Whether every item is `True`; stops at the first `False`.
@intrinsic("all")
pure def all(xs: Iterable[bool]) -> bool

## The absolute value; `abs` of the smallest `int` panics with an overflow.
@intrinsic("abs")
pure def abs[T: Num](x: T) -> T

## Panics with `message`, unwinding the task (05, "Panics").
@intrinsic("panic")
pure def panic(message: str) -> Never

## Panics with "entered unreachable code".
@intrinsic("unreachable")
pure def unreachable(message: str = "") -> Never

## A typed hole (D-061): checks as the type its position expects; running it
## stops the program there.
@intrinsic("todo")
pure def todo[T](name: str = "") -> T

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ int, f64, bool, char

impl int:
    ## `Less`, `Equal` or `Greater`.
    @intrinsic("int.cmp")
    pure def cmp(self, other: int) -> Ordering
    ## The nearest `f64`; exact up to 2**53.
    @intrinsic("int.to_f64")
    pure def to_f64(self) -> f64
    ## `self` raised to `exp`, which must not be negative; overflow panics.
    @intrinsic("int.pow")
    pure def pow(self, exp: int) -> int

impl f64:
    ## `Less`, `Equal` or `Greater`; panics if either is NaN.
    @intrinsic("f64.cmp")
    pure def cmp(self, other: f64) -> Ordering
    @intrinsic("f64.sqrt")
    pure def sqrt(self) -> f64
    @intrinsic("f64.floor")
    pure def floor(self) -> f64
    @intrinsic("f64.ceil")
    pure def ceil(self) -> f64
    ## Rounds half away from zero.
    @intrinsic("f64.round")
    pure def round(self) -> f64
    @intrinsic("f64.abs")
    pure def abs(self) -> f64
    ## The integer part; panics if the value is NaN or out of range for `int`.
    @intrinsic("f64.truncate_to_int")
    pure def truncate_to_int(self) -> int
    @intrinsic("f64.is_nan")
    pure def is_nan(self) -> bool

impl bool:
    @intrinsic("bool.cmp")
    pure def cmp(self, other: bool) -> Ordering

impl char:
    @intrinsic("char.cmp")
    pure def cmp(self, other: char) -> Ordering
    ## Whether the character is an ASCII digit `0`..`9`.
    @intrinsic("char.is_digit")
    pure def is_digit(self) -> bool
    ## The value of an ASCII digit, or `None`.
    @intrinsic("char.to_digit")
    pure def to_digit(self) -> int?
    @intrinsic("char.is_alphabetic")
    pure def is_alphabetic(self) -> bool
    @intrinsic("char.is_whitespace")
    pure def is_whitespace(self) -> bool
    @intrinsic("char.is_uppercase")
    pure def is_uppercase(self) -> bool
    @intrinsic("char.is_lowercase")
    pure def is_lowercase(self) -> bool

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ str and String

impl str:
    @intrinsic("str.cmp")
    pure def cmp(self, other: str) -> Ordering
    @intrinsic("str.is_empty")
    pure def is_empty(self) -> bool
    ## The Unicode scalar values.
    @intrinsic("str.chars")
    pure def chars(self) -> Iter[char]
    ## The lines, split at `\n` with a trailing `\r` removed; no final empty line.
    @intrinsic("str.lines")
    pure def lines(self) -> Iter[str]
    ## The runs of non-whitespace characters.
    @intrinsic("str.split_whitespace")
    pure def split_whitespace(self) -> Iter[str]
    ## The pieces between occurrences of `sep`, which must not be empty.
    @intrinsic("str.split")
    pure def split(self, sep: str) -> Iter[str]
    ## The text before and after the first `sep`, or `None`.
    @intrinsic("str.split_once")
    pure def split_once(self, sep: str) -> (str, str)?
    @intrinsic("str.trim")
    pure def trim(self) -> str
    @intrinsic("str.trim_start")
    pure def trim_start(self) -> str
    @intrinsic("str.trim_end")
    pure def trim_end(self) -> str
    @intrinsic("str.starts_with")
    pure def starts_with(self, prefix: str) -> bool
    @intrinsic("str.ends_with")
    pure def ends_with(self, suffix: str) -> bool
    @intrinsic("str.contains")
    pure def contains(self, part: str) -> bool
    ## The text without `prefix`, or the whole text if it does not start with it.
    @intrinsic("str.strip_prefix")
    pure def strip_prefix(self, prefix: str) -> str
    ## The text without `suffix`, or the whole text if it does not end with it.
    @intrinsic("str.strip_suffix")
    pure def strip_suffix(self, suffix: str) -> str
    ## The index in characters of the first occurrence of `part`, or `None`.
    @intrinsic("str.find")
    pure def find(self, part: str) -> int?
    @intrinsic("str.replace")
    pure def replace(self, old: str, new: str) -> String
    ## Unicode lower-case mapping.
    @intrinsic("str.to_lowercase")
    pure def to_lowercase(self) -> String
    @intrinsic("str.to_uppercase")
    pure def to_uppercase(self) -> String
    @intrinsic("str.to_string")
    pure def to_string(self) -> String
    ## The decimal integer the whole text spells, with an optional sign, or `None`.
    @intrinsic("str.parse_int")
    pure def parse_int(self) -> int?
    ## `self` repeated `n` times.
    @intrinsic("str.repeat")
    pure def repeat(self, n: int) -> String

impl String:
    ## A new string: empty, or a copy of `text`.
    @intrinsic("String.new")
    pure def new(text: str = "") -> String
    ## Appends `text`.
    @intrinsic("String.push")
    pure def push(mut self, text: str)
    @intrinsic("String.push_char")
    pure def push_char(mut self, c: char)
    @intrinsic("String.clear")
    pure def clear(mut self)

impl Path:
    @intrinsic("Path.new")
    pure def new(text: str) -> Path
    ## The path as text.
    @intrinsic("Path.as_str")
    pure def as_str(self) -> str

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ collections

impl[T] list[T]:
    @intrinsic("list.new")
    pure def new() -> list[T]
    @intrinsic("list.with_capacity")
    pure def with_capacity(n: int) -> list[T]
    ## Appends `x` at the end.
    @intrinsic("list.push")
    @keeps(self, x)
    pure def push(mut self, own x: T)
    ## Removes and returns the last element, or `None`.
    @intrinsic("list.pop")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def pop(mut self) -> T?
    ## Inserts `x` before index `i`, which may equal the length.
    @intrinsic("list.insert")
    @keeps(self, x)
    pure def insert(mut self, i: int, own x: T)
    ## Removes and returns the element at `i`; panics if out of bounds.
    @intrinsic("list.remove")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def remove(mut self, i: int) -> T
    @intrinsic("list.is_empty")
    pure def is_empty(self) -> bool
    ## Whether an element equals `x`.
    @structural("list.contains")
    pure def contains(self, x: T) -> bool where T: Eq:
        for y in self:
            if y == x:
                return True
        return False
    ## The element at `i`, or `None` when out of bounds.
    @intrinsic("list.get")
    pure def get(self, i: int) -> ref T?
    @intrinsic("list.first")
    pure def first(self) -> ref T?
    @intrinsic("list.last")
    pure def last(self) -> ref T?
    ## Borrows each element in order.
    @intrinsic("list.iter")
    pure def iter(self) -> Iter[ref T]
    ## Sorts in ascending order; stable.
    @structural("list.sort")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def sort(mut self) where T: Ord:
        self.sort_by((a, b) -> a.cmp(b))
    ## Sorts by `cmp`; stable. A merge sort: runs of one element, merged
    ## pairwise until one is left.
    pure def sort_by(mut self, cmp: pure (ref T, ref T) -> Ordering):
        if len(self) < 2:
            return
        var runs: list[list[T]] = []
        while not self.is_empty():
            runs.push([self.pop().expect("an element")])
        runs.reverse()
        while len(runs) > 1:
            var merged: list[list[T]] = []
            if len(runs) % 2 == 1:
                merged.push(runs.pop().expect("a run"))
            while len(runs) >= 2:
                let later = runs.pop().expect("a run")
                let earlier = runs.pop().expect("a run")
                merged.push(merge_runs(earlier, later, cmp))
            merged.reverse()
            runs = merged
        self.extend(runs.pop().expect("a run"))
    @intrinsic("list.reverse")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def reverse(mut self)
    @intrinsic("list.clear")
    pure def clear(mut self)
    ## Moves the elements of `other` to the end.
    @intrinsic("list.extend")
    @keeps(self, other)
    pure def extend(mut self, own other: list[T])
    ## The `Display` renderings of the elements, separated by `sep`.
    @structural("list.join")
    pure def join(self, sep: str) -> String where T: Display:
        var out = String.new()
        var first = True
        for x in self:
            if not first:
                out.push(sep)
            first = False
            out.push("{x}")
        return out
    ## The sum of the elements, 0 for none; integer overflow panics. Floats
    ## are summed pairwise in a fixed shape (D-074).
    @intrinsic("sum")
    pure def sum(self) -> T where T: Num

impl[K, V] dict[K, V]:
    @intrinsic("dict.new")
    pure def new() -> dict[K, V]
    @intrinsic("dict.get")
    pure def get(self, key: K) -> ref V?
    ## Inserts or replaces; returns the previous value.
    @intrinsic("dict.insert")
    @keeps(self, value)
    pure def insert(mut self, own key: K, own value: V) -> V?
    @intrinsic("dict.remove")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def remove(mut self, key: K) -> V?
    @intrinsic("dict.contains")
    pure def contains(self, key: K) -> bool
    @intrinsic("dict.is_empty")
    pure def is_empty(self) -> bool
    ## The slot for `key`, filled or not.
    @intrinsic("dict.entry")
    pure def entry(mut self, own key: K) -> Entry[K, V]
    @intrinsic("dict.keys")
    pure def keys(self) -> Iter[ref K]
    @intrinsic("dict.values")
    pure def values(self) -> Iter[ref V]
    ## The entries in insertion order.
    @intrinsic("dict.items")
    pure def items(self) -> Iter[(ref K, ref V)]

impl[K, V] Entry[K, V]:
    ## The value in the slot, after storing `value` there if it was empty.
    @intrinsic("Entry.or_insert")
    pure def or_insert(own self, own value: V) -> ref mut V

impl[T] set[T]:
    @intrinsic("set.new")
    pure def new() -> set[T]
    ## Adds `x`; returns whether it was new.
    @intrinsic("set.add")
    pure def add(mut self, own x: T) -> bool
    @intrinsic("set.remove")
    pure def remove(mut self, x: T) -> bool
    @intrinsic("set.contains")
    pure def contains(self, x: T) -> bool
    @intrinsic("set.is_empty")
    pure def is_empty(self) -> bool
    @intrinsic("set.iter")
    pure def iter(self) -> Iter[ref T]

impl[T] Iter[T]:
    ## The next item, or `None` at the end.
    @intrinsic("Iter.next")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def next(mut self) -> T?

## The elements of two sorted runs in one sorted run; of equal elements,
## `earlier`'s come first (`list.sort_by`).
pure def merge_runs[T](own earlier: list[T], own later: list[T], cmp: pure (ref T, ref T) -> Ordering) -> list[T]:
    var a = earlier
    var b = later
    var out: list[T] = []
    # From the back: the larger last element, `later`'s when equal.
    while not a.is_empty() and not b.is_empty():
        if cmp(ref b[^1], ref a[^1]) != Less:
            out.push(b.pop().expect("an element"))
        else:
            out.push(a.pop().expect("an element"))
    while not a.is_empty():
        out.push(a.pop().expect("an element"))
    while not b.is_empty():
        out.push(b.pop().expect("an element"))
    out.reverse()
    return out

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ iterator adapters

## `it.take(n)`: at most `left` more items of `inner`.
struct Take[I]:
    inner: I
    left: int

impl[I: Iterator] Iterator for Take[I]:
    type Item = I.Item
    def next(mut self) -> I.Item?:
        if self.left <= 0:
            return None
        self.left -= 1
        return self.inner.next()

## `it.skip(n)`: the items of `inner` after the first `left`.
struct Skip[I]:
    inner: I
    left: int

impl[I: Iterator] Iterator for Skip[I]:
    type Item = I.Item
    def next(mut self) -> I.Item?:
        while self.left > 0:
            self.left -= 1
            if self.inner.next().is_none():
                return None
        return self.inner.next()

## `it.map(f)`: each item of `inner` passed through `f`. A view, since `f`
## may borrow what it captures.
view struct Map[I: Iterator, U]:
    inner: I
    f: (I.Item) -> U

impl[I: Iterator, U] Iterator for Map[I, U]:
    type Item = U
    def next(mut self) -> U?:
        match self.inner.next():
            case Some(x):
                return Some(self.f(x))
            case None:
                return None

## `it.filter(keep)`: the items of `inner` for which `keep` is `True`.
view struct Filter[I: Iterator]:
    inner: I
    keep: (ref I.Item) -> bool

impl[I: Iterator] Iterator for Filter[I]:
    type Item = I.Item
    def next(mut self) -> I.Item?:
        while True:
            match self.inner.next():
                case Some(x):
                    if self.keep(ref x):
                        return Some(x)
                case None:
                    return None

## `it.enumerate()`: each item of `inner` with its index.
struct Enumerate[I]:
    inner: I
    index: int

impl[I: Iterator] Iterator for Enumerate[I]:
    type Item = (int, I.Item)
    def next(mut self) -> (int, I.Item)?:
        match self.inner.next():
            case Some(x):
                let i = self.index
                self.index += 1
                return Some((i, x))
            case None:
                return None

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ shared ownership, cells, mutexes, arenas

# The fields and methods of the value are reached through a `Shared`
# directly, so its own functions take it as an argument (`Shared.count(s)`)
# rather than as `self`, and never hide the value's methods.
impl[T] Shared[T]:
    ## The first owner of `value`.
    @intrinsic("Shared.new")
    pure def new(own value: T) -> Shared[T]
    ## The value itself.
    @intrinsic("Shared.get")
    pure def get(s: Shared[T]) -> ref T
    ## A `Weak` handle on the value.
    @intrinsic("Shared.downgrade")
    pure def downgrade(s: Shared[T]) -> Weak[T]
    ## The number of owners.
    @intrinsic("Shared.count")
    pure def count(s: Shared[T]) -> int

impl[T] Weak[T]:
    ## Another owner of the value, if it has one still.
    @intrinsic("Weak.upgrade")
    pure def upgrade(self) -> Shared[T]?

impl[T] Cell[T]:
    @intrinsic("Cell.new")
    pure def new(own value: T) -> Cell[T]
    ## A copy of the value.
    @intrinsic("Cell.get")
    pure def get(self) -> T where T: Copy
    ## Replaces the value, dropping the old one.
    @intrinsic("Cell.set")
    def set(self, own value: T)
    ## Replaces the value and returns the old one.
    @intrinsic("Cell.replace")
    def replace(self, own value: T) -> T

impl[T] Mutex[T]:
    @intrinsic("Mutex.new")
    pure def new(own value: T) -> Mutex[T]
    ## The lock, until the guard is dropped; panics if the mutex is held
    ## (stage 0 has one task) or poisoned.
    @intrinsic("Mutex.lock")
    def lock(self) -> MutexGuard[T]
    ## Whether a guard dropped by unwinding left the value's invariant broken.
    @intrinsic("Mutex.is_poisoned")
    pure def is_poisoned(self) -> bool

impl[T] Arena[T]:
    @intrinsic("Arena.new")
    pure def new() -> Arena[T]
    ## Stores `value` and returns its key.
    @intrinsic("Arena.insert")
    @keeps(self, value)
    pure def insert(mut self, own value: T) -> Key[T]
    ## The value of `key`, or `None` if the key is stale.
    @intrinsic("Arena.get")
    pure def get(self, key: Key[T]) -> ref T?
    @intrinsic("Arena.get_mut")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def get_mut(mut self, key: Key[T]) -> ref mut T?
    ## Drops the value of `key`, if it is there; the key goes stale.
    @intrinsic("Arena.remove")
    pure def remove(mut self, key: Key[T])
    ## Takes the value of `key` out, if it is there; the key goes stale.
    @intrinsic("Arena.take")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def take(mut self, key: Key[T]) -> T?
    @intrinsic("Arena.contains")
    pure def contains(self, key: Key[T]) -> bool
    ## The keys of the values, in slot order.
    @intrinsic("Arena.keys")
    pure def keys(self) -> Iter[Key[T]]

impl[T] Len for Arena[T]:
    @intrinsic("len")
    pure def len(self) -> int

impl[T: Debug] Debug for Shared[T]:
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter):
        Shared.get(self).fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[T: Display] Display for Shared[T]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        Shared.get(self).fmt(mut f)

impl[T: Eq] Eq for Shared[T]:
    pure def eq(self, other: Shared[T]) -> bool:
        return Shared.get(self) == Shared.get(other)

impl[T] Debug for Weak[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl[T] Debug for Cell[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl[T] Debug for Mutex[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl[T] Debug for MutexGuard[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl[T] Debug for Arena[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Option and Result

impl[T] Option[T]:
    @intrinsic("Option.is_some")
    pure def is_some(self) -> bool
    @intrinsic("Option.is_none")
    pure def is_none(self) -> bool
    ## The value; panics with `message` if there is none.
    @intrinsic("Option.expect")
    pure def expect(own self, message: str) -> T
    ## The value, or `default`.
    @intrinsic("Option.or")
    pure def or(own self, own default: T) -> T
    ## `f` of the value, if there is one.
    def map[U](own self, f: (T) -> U) -> U?:
        if self.is_none():
            return None
        return Some(f(self.expect("a value")))

impl[T, E] Result[T, E]:
    @intrinsic("Result.is_ok")
    pure def is_ok(self) -> bool
    @intrinsic("Result.is_err")
    pure def is_err(self) -> bool
    ## The value; panics with `message` and the error if there is an error.
    @intrinsic("Result.expect")
    pure def expect(own self, message: str) -> T
    ## The value, or `default`.
    @intrinsic("Result.or")
    pure def or(own self, own default: T) -> T
    @intrinsic("Result.ok")
    pure def ok(own self) -> T?
    @intrinsic("Result.err")
    pure def err(own self) -> E?
    ## The error, if any, wrapped with what the program was doing:
    ## `fs.read_to_string(p).context("loading {p}")` (05, "Context and
    ## traces"; a std convention, not syntax).
    pure def context(own self, message: str) -> T ! Contextual where E: Display:
        if self.is_ok():
            return Ok(self.expect("a value"))
        let cause = self.err().expect("an error")
        return Err(Context(message=message.to_string(), cause="{cause}"))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ capabilities

impl Fs:
    ## The whole file as text; `NotFound`, `PermissionDenied` or `InvalidData`
    ## (not UTF-8) on failure.
    @intrinsic("Fs.read_to_string")
    def read_to_string(self, path: Path) -> String ! IoError
    ## Creates or replaces the file with `text`.
    @intrinsic("Fs.write_text")
    def write_text(self, path: Path, text: str) -> ! IoError
    @intrinsic("Fs.exists")
    def exists(self, path: Path) -> bool
    ## The same file system with every write refused as `PermissionDenied`.
    @intrinsic("Fs.read_only")
    pure def read_only(self) -> Fs
    ## The same file system limited to `dir`; paths are relative to it and
    ## may not leave it.
    @intrinsic("Fs.restrict_to")
    pure def restrict_to(self, dir: Path) -> Fs
    ## A fake file system in memory, holding `files` (17, "Fixtures").
    @intrinsic("Fs.in_memory")
    pure def in_memory(files: dict[str, str] = {}) -> Fs

impl Env:
    ## The command-line arguments after the program's own name.
    @intrinsic("Env.args")
    def args(self) -> list[str]
    ## The variable `name`, or `None`.
    @intrinsic("Env.get")
    def get(self, name: str) -> String?

impl Clock:
    ## The current time.
    @intrinsic("Clock.now")
    def now(self) -> DateTime

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the traits of the builtin types
# The leaf types (`int`, `f64`, `bool`, `char`, `str`, `String`, `Path`,
# `DateTime`, `()`) and tuples of up to six elements implement `Display`,
# `Debug`, `Eq`, `PartialOrd`, `Ord` (not `f64`) and `Hash` (not `f64`) in
# the generated module `<std>/builtin_impls.sisu` (see
# compiler/crates/sisu-resolve/src/std_sources.rs), with the intrinsics
# `structural.*`, whose contracts are written there.

impl[T: Debug] Debug for list[T]:
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter):
        f.write("[")
        f.depth += 1
        var first = True
        for x in self:
            if not first:
                f.write(", ")
            first = False
            x.fmt_debug(mut f)
        f.depth -= 1
        f.write("]")

impl[T: Debug] Display for list[T]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[T: Eq] Eq for list[T]:
    pure def eq(self, other: list[T]) -> bool:
        if len(self) != len(other):
            return False
        for i in range(len(self)):
            if self[i] != other[i]:
                return False
        return True

impl[T: PartialOrd] PartialOrd for list[T]:
    pure def partial_cmp(self, other: list[T]) -> Ordering?:
        var i = 0
        while i < len(self) and i < len(other):
            let c = self[i].partial_cmp(other[i])
            if c != Some(Equal):
                return c
            i += 1
        return Some(len(self).cmp(len(other)))

impl[T: Ord] Ord for list[T]:
    pure def cmp(self, other: list[T]) -> Ordering:
        var i = 0
        while i < len(self) and i < len(other):
            let c = self[i].cmp(other[i])
            if c != Equal:
                return c
            i += 1
        return len(self).cmp(len(other))

impl[T: Hash] Hash for list[T]:
    pure def hash(self, mut h: Hasher):
        h.write_int(len(self))
        for x in self:
            x.hash(mut h)

impl[T] Len for list[T]:
    @intrinsic("len")
    pure def len(self) -> int

impl[T: Debug] Debug for set[T]:
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter):
        if len(self) == 0:
            f.write("set()")
            return
        f.write("{{")
        f.depth += 1
        var first = True
        for x in self:
            if not first:
                f.write(", ")
            first = False
            x.fmt_debug(mut f)
        f.depth -= 1
        f.write("}}")

impl[T: Debug] Display for set[T]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[T: Hash] Eq for set[T]:
    pure def eq(self, other: set[T]) -> bool:
        if len(self) != len(other):
            return False
        for x in self:
            if not other.contains(x):
                return False
        return True

impl[T] Len for set[T]:
    @intrinsic("len")
    pure def len(self) -> int

impl[K: Debug, V: Debug] Debug for dict[K, V]:
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter):
        f.write("{{")
        f.depth += 1
        var first = True
        for k, v in self:
            if not first:
                f.write(", ")
            first = False
            k.fmt_debug(mut f)
            f.write(": ")
            v.fmt_debug(mut f)
        f.depth -= 1
        f.write("}}")

impl[K: Debug, V: Debug] Display for dict[K, V]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[K: Hash, V: Eq] Eq for dict[K, V]:
    pure def eq(self, other: dict[K, V]) -> bool:
        if len(self) != len(other):
            return False
        for k, v in self:
            let Some(w) = other.get(k) else:
                return False
            if v != w:
                return False
        return True

impl[K, V] Len for dict[K, V]:
    @intrinsic("len")
    pure def len(self) -> int

impl[T: Debug] Display for Option[T]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[T: Debug, E: Debug] Display for Result[T, E]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl Display for Ordering:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[T: Debug] Debug for Box[T]:
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.boxed.fmt_debug(mut f)

impl[T: Display] Display for Box[T]:
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter):
        self.boxed.fmt(mut f)

impl[T] Iterator for Iter[T]:
    type Item = T
    @intrinsic("Iter.next")
    @keeps(self)
    pure def next(mut self) -> T?

impl[T] Debug for Iter[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl[T] Display for Iter[T]:
    @intrinsic("structural.display")
    pure def fmt(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl[K, V] Debug for Entry[K, V]:
    @intrinsic("structural.debug")
    pure def fmt_debug(self, mut f: Formatter)

impl FromLiteral[str] for String:
    @intrinsic("str.to_string")
    pure def from_literal(literal: str) -> String

impl FromLiteral[str] for Path:
    @intrinsic("Path.new")
    pure def from_literal(literal: str) -> Path

impl Num for int:
    pass

impl Num for f64:
    pass
